//! Function names by address, generated. Do not edit.
//!
//! Emitted by `tools/symbols.py` from the linker's `/MAP` output, which
//! is produced by the `link-arg=/MAP` in `.cargo/config.toml`. The
//! image itself is stripped -- its COFF symbol table has four entries --
//! so this is the only way a fault report can name the function it is in.
//!
//! **Sorted by address, and `lookup` binary-searches it.** A fault
//! handler runs with interrupts off on a machine that is about to halt,
//! so this allocates nothing and takes no lock.
//!
//! Names are demangled by a heuristic that is exact for plain functions
//! and approximate for impl methods on generic types. Anything that
//! would not parse is left mangled rather than guessed at.

/// Identifies this symbol table, and therefore the code layout it describes.
///
/// **Printed beside a resolved name, because a table is only valid for its
/// own build.** An rva photographed off one machine and resolved against a
/// later image names whatever now lives at that offset, confidently and
/// wrongly -- which happened twice while building this: 0xaa013 was
/// `dev::power::hwp_range` before a one-line fix and `mine::checks` after.
///
/// A hash of the table's own contents rather than the linker's timestamp.
/// The timestamp was the first attempt and it cannot work: every link
/// produces a new one, so the value never converges, and the table is
/// generated from the map of the *previous* link -- so it named a build
/// that was not the one running. Hashing the contents means two builds of
/// the same code carry the same stamp, which is the question being asked.
pub const BUILD_STAMP: u32 = 0xD65BCF5;

/// Where the image was linked to load. An address below this is not ours.
pub const IMAGE_BASE: u64 = 0x140000000;

